-- Q3901: SOC Reporting [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Per SSAE No. 18 (AT-C Section 320.09), management of the service organization is responsible for establishing the control objectives in a SOC 1 report. These objectives describe what controls are intended to achieve in relation to user entities'' internal control over financial reporting. Management defines the scope; the service auditor evaluates whether controls meet those objectives.
Wrong (A): The service auditor evaluates whether controls are suitably designed and operating effectively against management-defined objectives but does not define them.
Wrong (B): User entities'' external auditors are consumers of the SOC 1 report, but SSAE 18 does not assign them the responsibility of establishing control objectives.
Wrong (D): SSAE 18 does not require a joint committee — control objective responsibility rests solely with the service organization''s management.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3901;

-- Q3902: SOC Reporting [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Per the AICPA Guide: SOC 2 Reporting on an Examination of Controls, Section 4.28, a SOC report reflects the system and controls during a specific examination period. The six-month gap means there is no independent assurance that controls operated effectively during July through December 2025. Edgewater''s auditors may need a bridge letter, additional procedures, or management representations to cover the gap.
Wrong (A): SOC 2 reports do not provide perpetual assurance — their relevance diminishes as time passes because changes to controls, personnel, or systems may have occurred.
Wrong (B): The user entity''s auditors must consider whether the report covers the relevant period, not merely verify that a SOC 2 report exists.
Wrong (C): Gaps are not automatically unacceptable — auditors can use bridge letters and additional procedures rather than requiring a different vendor.$EXPL$
