-- Q3903: SOC Reporting [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Per AT-C Section 205.72, a qualified opinion results from identified control deficiencies. To resolve it, the organization should remediate the underlying deficiencies, implement improved procedures, and demonstrate consistent operation throughout the next examination period. Sustained remediation over a full period is required.
Wrong (A): Switching auditing firms does not remediate the underlying deficiency — the new auditor would likely identify the same issues if they remain unresolved.
Wrong (C): Requesting the auditor to retroactively change an opinion violates professional standards — opinions are based on evidence gathered during the examination.
Wrong (D): Ceasing the qualified activities creates a control environment gap rather than addressing the root cause of the exceptions.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3903;

-- Q3906: SOC Reporting [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Per AT-C Section 205.A45, SOC 2 reports are restricted-use reports intended only for parties who have sufficient knowledge and a legitimate business need — typically management, user entities, and their auditors. A potential investor without a service relationship would not typically qualify for access.
Wrong (A): SOC 2 reports cannot be shared with anyone — they carry explicit distribution restrictions unlike general-use SOC 3 reports.
Wrong (B): Issuance does not remove distribution restrictions — the restricted-use designation persists regardless of when the report was completed.
Wrong (D): Distribution is not limited solely to current user entities — prospective user entities and their auditors may also qualify if they have a legitimate business need.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3906;

