-- Q4906: Change Management [easy/L2]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): A change request form (or request for change, RFC) is the formal document that initiates the change management process per ITIL and COBIT frameworks. It captures the proposed modification, business justification, risk assessment, and expected impact before any development work begins.
Wrong (A): A post-implementation review report is completed after a change has been deployed, not before work begins — it evaluates whether the change achieved its objectives.
Wrong (B): A release deployment plan addresses how approved changes are moved to production, which occurs after the change has been developed, tested, and approved.
Wrong (C): A configuration audit checklist verifies that configuration baselines are accurate, not that a new change has been formally proposed and justified.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4906;

-- Q4907: Change Management [easy/L2]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Separation of duties in change management requires that the person who develops code is not the same person who deploys it to production. Per COBIT and ITIL best practices, a separate operations or release management team member should handle production deployments to prevent unauthorized modifications and reduce fraud risk.
Wrong (A): Allowing the developer who wrote the patch to deploy it violates separation of duties, creating risk that unauthorized or untested changes reach production.
Wrong (C): The end user who reported the defect typically lacks the technical access, authorization, and expertise required for production deployments.
Wrong (D): The project manager who approved the budget has a financial oversight role and typically does not have the technical access or operational responsibility for deployments.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4907;

